#
# the following was contributed by Bernt Gombala
# (file wc_near_rational_cases.txt)
#
# Hard-to-round cases for binary64 pow(X,Y) identified by taking
# X as a number equal to 1.0 plus/minus up to 256 ulps
# and taking Y as a rational number (A/B) plus/minus up to 128 ulps,
# with A,B picked in the range -128 to +128. All such cases with at least
# 90 identical bits after the round-bit are provided - there
# are 1443 such cases.
#  * For Y close to -1/4, this results in near-exact values with up to 153
#    identical bits after the round-bit. Such cases are obtained by taking
#    X = 1.0 plus/minus 8*N ulps and Y = -0.25 minus/plus 5*N ulps
#    for small integer values of N.
#  * For other near-rational values of Y, near-exact cases and
#    near-midpoint values with up to 107 identical bits after
#    the round-bit have been found.
#
0x1.ffffffffffffdp-1,0x1.5555555555555p-1 # h=158
#
# the following was contributed by Bernt Gombala
# (file wc_subnorm_non_midpoint_cases.txt)
#
# Cases for binary64 pow(x,y) that are neither exact nor midpoint, but
# instead have the property that if they were to be evaluated in a format
# that is the usual binary64 except having a wider exponent-range, then
# they would have been midpoint-cases - which makes them susceptible to
# double-rounding problems for implementations that compute a result with
# binary64-mantissa precision first, then coverts that down to subnormal
# afterwards. All of these cases evaluate to binary64 subnormal results
# in the range (2^-1026,2^-1022) - there exists 445 such cases.
#
